{
  "source": "Parsed exactly from walk-down-cycle (4).svg's <text> coordinates — supersedes walk-down-cycle (3).svg. EIGHT groups this time — <g class=\"body|head|armL|armR|legL|legR|shoeL|shoeR\"> — head split out from body, plus shoeL/shoeR layered over the legs. Each group is independently translateY-shifted per quarter-step via its own @keyframes block (steps(1,end), 0.64s/4-phase cadence), and shoeL/shoeR ALSO toggle opacity per quarter (shoeL hidden at frame 3, shoeR hidden at frame 1) — the source's way of tucking a shoe out of sight behind the leg mid-stride, since this bake has no z-order/overlap compositing beyond paint order. Baked into the same 4-discrete-frame shape the game expects by reading each group's own @keyframes translateY AND opacity values directly (not hand-transcribed) at t=0/25/50/75% — an opacity-0 group is skipped entirely for that frame — and compositing in the SVG's own paint order (body -> head -> armL -> armR -> legL -> legR -> shoeL -> shoeR) into one grid per frame, padded 1 rows at the top for the biggest upward shift. HEAD-BOB OVERRIDE: this is the first walk-down reference to split the head out of the body into its own group; the source has \"body\" (the torso) sit completely still while only \"head\" bobs, which reads as the head stretching away from a fixed neck rather than the whole upper body rising like every earlier bake did. Per request, body's offsets are overridden to follow head's exactly ([0,-20,0,-20]px) so they move as one unit again, matching how (1)/(2)/(3) — which never split head from body — always animated it. GROUND-NORMALIZED for consistency with every walk-down bake so far, though this particular reference never actually needs it: only one leg ever lifts at a time (the other is always at its 0px rest offset), so every frame already has a foot on the ground row — unlike (2)/(3), which both required the shift. Like (1), frames 0 and 2 are full-rest poses with both legs down (though arms still move a little in between, unlike (1)); (3) is the only walk-down bake so far where every frame has leg motion. Colours clustered across all eight groups together down to 40 (custom JS k-means++, no deps) from 286 near-unique source hexes, then every column doubled for this game's non-square character cell — see PLAYER's comment in sprites.ts for the same fix.",
